        {
          "question": "e-9482",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}3\u0394{x}_{i}\u0394{y}_{j} & =3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\u0394{x}_{i}\u0394{y}_{j} \\\\  & =3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\u0394{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m\\Delta x_i$ and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[-1, 2]$ and $[2,3]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i} & =2-(-1)=3, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\u0394{y}_{j} & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\sum_{i=1}^m \\Delta x_i \\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta y_j = 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1 = 9.\n$$",
